{"author":{"id":"7674679dac9170669f752d932f8b5955b1d55717359e13b1c1e687003c7bb99f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789854033,"nonce":"CyuChPiiYvHZfWrYJ+gOkgxf670=","title":"Task: Route non-object git operations through the git CLI","message":"`git work pull` fails against an SSH remote with:\n\n    ssh: handshake failed: ssh: unable to authenticate,\n    attempted methods [none publickey], no supported methods remain\n\ngo-git's transport ignores ~/.ssh/config and the default identity files; its\nDefaultAuthBuilder (plumbing/transport/ssh/common.go) only ever offers keys\nheld by ssh-agent. An empty agent therefore breaks fetch/push even though the\ngit CLI authenticates fine. This is the same class of defect as upstream #1475\n(go-git not evaluating [include]/[includeIf]), fixed in our 68abc13 by routing\nconfig reads through `git config`.\n\nUnify both workarounds instead of accumulating one-offs: a single exec-backed\nrepository.ClockedRepo decorator that owns every operation where go-git\nreimplements git's environment — config resolution and remote transport —\nwhile local object access (objects, trees, refs, clocks, browse) stays on\ngo-git, which is fast and correct there.\n\n- Rename package gitconfig to gitcli; factor the exec plumbing (dir, argv,\n  exit codes, stderr) into one runner shared by config and remote.\n- FetchRefs/PushRefs run `git fetch`/`git push` with the same refspecs\n  go-git built. PushRefs keeps updating refs/remotes/\u003cremote\u003e/* by passing a\n  matching fetch refspec with -c, the CLI equivalent of go-git's in-memory\n  remote-config hack. url.insteadOf resolution comes free with the CLI.\n- The pristine repository package stays untouched; if git is not on PATH the\n  repo is returned unwrapped and go-git's behaviour remains.","files":null},{"type":9,"timestamp":1789854033,"nonce":"ujZyj1E7XwjJUZvgjSEF8blJvJU=","key":"type","value":"task"},{"type":9,"timestamp":1789854033,"nonce":"DWX2YQp/8pRx85Ik+EacTJxTEFo=","key":"status","value":"to-do"}]}